52914d82","cmd-ad3bdb3ecb0b4171","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The retained root-only v1 database and original-script copies were bounded recovery prerequisites; the replacement script and live migration directly implemented the task. Failure and constraint probes used a disposable directory or rolled-back transactions, generated bytecode was removed, and no SQLite sidecars remained. The reboot was required by the provider-wide evidence protocol. Final lifecycle evidence shows no failed services, new exposure, or package damage.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c8b25d7fe80444e4","cmd-72980ec352914d82","cmd-79f32f8192dd4d8b","cmd-2d8291e45c684982","cmd-61a121e3a36e4d00","cmd-b73fcc1dda6149be","cmd-8493953358644d32","cmd-40a7aacdcea341f8","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The mandatory node-restart protocol is also complete: node1 was healthy before restart, the restart was acknowledged and observed through connection loss, its boot ID changed, and system, catalog, migration, data, OS, and kernel health were verified after recovery. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["Failure rollback was tested against a disposable copy of the captured v1 database rather than by deliberately failing the production catalog; the copy used the repaired migration unchanged and directly demonstrated transactional rollback behavior."]}
